%N A400279 The number of skipped edges in the regular (n+1)-gon when the regular (n+2)-gon is drawn in a particular polygon spiral (see comments).
%C A400279 The sequence is based on a spiral constructed from polygons with an increasing number of sides. Starting with a regular triangle, the polygons having an increasing number of edges are added one by one so that each of these regular polygons (the (n+1)-gon) shares exactly one edge with the previous polygon (the n-gon). The shared edge is chosen so that the (n+1)-gon is drawn as close as possible to the previously drawn polygons without overlapping them.
%C A400279 The definition of the polygon spiral is given by the following steps:
%C A400279 1. Draw a regular triangle: P(3)
%C A400279   Going clockwise around, the vertices are labeled as V(3,1), V(3,2) and V(3,3).
%C A400279 2. Draw a square: P(4)
%C A400279   The shared edge will be the one having the lowest indexes in the triangle, that is: [V(3,1), V(3,2)].
%C A400279   The vertices of the square are labeled as V(4,1), V(4,2), (V4,3) and V(4,4) in clockwise direction, so that the shared edge is [V(4,4), V(4,1)] in the square. That is, V(4,4)=V(3,2) and V(4,1)=V(3,1).
%C A400279 3. Draw a regular pentagon: P(5)
%C A400279   The shared edge will be the one having the lowest indexes in the square, that is: [V(4,1), V(4,2)]. The pentagon will not overlap with either the square or the triangle.
%C A400279   The vertices of the pentagon are labeled as V(5,1), V(5,2), (V5,3), V(5,4) and V(5,5) in clockwise direction, so that the edge shared with the square is [V(5,5), V(5,1)] in the pentagon. That is, V(5,5)=V(4,2) and V(5,1)=V(4,1).
%C A400279 4. Draw a regular hexagon: P(6)
%C A400279   The shared edge in the pentagon cannot be the one having the lowest indexes because the regular hexagon drawn at the [V(5,1), V(5,2)] edge would overlap with the P(3) triangle. Therefore that edge has to be skipped and the next lowest indexed edge has to be used: [V(5,2), V(5,3)]. There is no overlapping when using that edge as the shared edge. The vertices of the hexagon are labeled as V(6,1), V(6,2), (V6,3), V(6,4), V(6,5) and V(6,6) in clockwise direction, so that the edge shared with the pentagon is [V(6,6), V(6,1)] in the hexagon and [V(5,3), V(5,2)] in the pentagon.
%C A400279 ...
%C A400279 n. Draw a regular (n+2)-gon: P(n+2)
%C A400279   In the (n+1)-gon, the shared edge is the one with the lowest possible indexes that will generate the (n+2)-gon without overlapping any previously drawn polygons. The vertices of the (n+2)-gon are labeled as V(n+2,1), V(n+2,2), ..., V(n+2,n+2) in clockwise direction, so that the edge shared with the (n+1)-gon is [V(n+2,n+2), V(n+2,1)].
%C A400279 The n-th value of the sequence, a(n), is defined as the number of skipped edges in the (n+1)-gon when the (n+2)-gon is drawn.
%C A400279 Formal definition: a(1) = 0, a(n) = k where the edge shared between P(n+1) and P(n+2) is [V(n+1,k+1), V(n+1,k+2)] in P(n+1)
